# base ADR-registry tooling (`just adr <recipe>`).
#
# ADR-00000011: action-named namespaces, one per subject; `adr` is the
# registry under doc/adr/ and the references into it. Its sibling gate is
# script/test/drivers/adr_numbering.sh, which is where a defect in the
# registry is REPORTED; this namespace is where one is repaired.
#
# `set working-directory` mirrors script/test/justfile.test so recipes run
# from the repo root whether reached via the `adr` module or `just -f`.
set working-directory := '../..'

# source_file (not justfile) is this module's own file: inside a mod?'d
# module justfile resolves to the ROOT entry, so listing it would show the
# entry's namespaces instead of these recipes.
# Default: list available recipes
default:
    @just --justfile '{{source_file()}}' --list

# lifecycle: CREATES NOTHING to reclaim. Pure bash over the checkout -- no
# compose, no container, no project.
#
# just adr renumber <record> <number> -> move one ADR record to another
# number and rewrite every reference to it, in one step.
#
# <record> is the number when one record claims it (`just adr renumber 30
# 33`) and the filename when more than one does. The reference set is
# DERIVED from the tree, not listed: the tracked files are swept for the
# three reference forms, the ADR index's bare numbers are rewritten there
# and nowhere else, and the generated documents under doc/test are
# REGENERATED rather than edited -- a `@test` name carrying the number is
# a row in one of them.
#
# It refuses, before writing anything, a target number somebody already
# claims and a source number two records claim: with two claimants a bare
# `ADR-<n>` in prose cannot be attributed to either, so the tool says so
# instead of guessing. The repair for that state is to renumber on the
# branch, where the record is the only claimant, and merge afterwards.
# script/adr/renumber.sh's header argues all of it.
renumber *args:
    ./script/adr/renumber.sh {{args}}
